<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- BADAN is local-first: no third-party SDKs and no analytics providers. Ingestion,
	     scores and export generation are all on-device and open no connection.

	     CORRECTED (ADR-0011 / D-37). This block previously said "zero network calls" and
	     "nothing is ever transmitted off-device". That stopped being true when the AI coach
	     shipped (ADR-0010 / D-22), which required this correction before ship and never got
	     it. BADAN now reaches the network from exactly two of its own surfaces, both opt-in
	     and both default-off: the AI coach, and methodology telemetry.

	     CORRECTED AGAIN (ADR-0014 / D-44). The line above said "no accounts", and BADAN now
	     has one — optional, Sign in with Apple, and scoped to carrying a subscription between
	     platforms and running referrals. That makes THREE network surfaces, and it adds a
	     collected data type this file did not previously carry: an account identifier.

	     NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is now NON-EMPTY, and the reason is methodology telemetry
	     (ADR-0011 / D-37). A footprint carries no health value, no date and no identifier —
	     but it IS collected data sent off device, so it is declared rather than argued away.
	     Under-declaring is an App Review risk; over-declaring is not.

	     Declared as OtherUsageData, not HealthFitness: a footprint says which calculation
	     method a user disagreed with and in which direction, never what any reading was. If
	     that ever stops being true the declaration is the least of the problems, and there is
	     a test (testFootprintPayloadContainsNoHealthValue) that fails loudly first.

	     NOT tracking and NOT linked to identity, both truthfully: there is no identifier in
	     the payload at all, so there is nothing to link it to and nothing to track across
	     apps. The AI coach remains undeclared because it stores nothing — a query is proxied
	     to the model and not retained.

	     Reading/writing the app's own on-device SQLite databases and UserDefaults remains
	     on-device processing, not "collection," which Apple's spec defines as transmission
	     off the device (or to a third party on-device).

	     A2 (ADR-0009) reviewed and deliberately changed nothing here. Background delivery and
	     BGTaskScheduler let BADAN do the SAME on-device work at a different time; they move no
	     data off the device, add no third party, and introduce no required-reason API
	     (BGTaskScheduler and HKObserverQuery are not on Apple's required-reason list). The
	     widened HealthKit read set likewise adds no collected-data type, because none of it is
	     transmitted. Padding this file with declarations that "look responsible" would make it
	     less accurate, not more — its value is that every entry below is load-bearing.

	     The standing rule: a new entry belongs here only when the app genuinely calls a
	     required-reason API or genuinely transmits something. Re-check on each release. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- ADR-0014 / D-44. The account identifier.

		     Declared as UserID and as LINKED, both truthfully and neither reluctantly: the
		     server holds an account row keyed to a hash of Apple's subject claim, and that row
		     is by definition linked to the person who signed in. Arguing that a salted hash is
		     "not really an identifier" is exactly the kind of reasoning App Review is right to
		     distrust, and under-declaring is the expensive mistake here.

		     Purpose is AppFunctionality and nothing else. The account exists to answer "what
		     did this person buy, and on which platform" and to run referrals. It is NOT used
		     for analytics, NOT for personalisation, and NOT for advertising — and there is no
		     code that could, since the only fields the server keeps are a product id, an
		     expiry, a referral code and a count (see backend/api/accounts.py, and the test
		     that pins its column list).

		     Tracking stays false: the identifier is never joined with data from other
		     companies' apps or sites, never leaves this service, and no advertising
		     identifier is collected at all.

		     What is NOT declared, deliberately: name and email. The client requests neither
		     Apple scope (AppleSignInController leaves requestedScopes empty), so neither is
		     ever received, let alone transmitted or stored. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUsageData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Database.swift diskUsage() calls FileManager.attributesOfItem(atPath:) on the app's
		     own DB files (badan.db / badan_sensitive.db and -wal/-shm siblings) under
		     Application Support/BADAN to report on-device storage usage (PERF-122). File
		     timestamps of files inside the app's own container. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- FeatureFlags.swift and AppEnvironment.swift use UserDefaults.standard to store the
		     app's own preferences (onboarding-complete flag, device id, debug feature flags) —
		     never shared with or read from outside the app. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
